# 5. HTR/OCR Engine

This is one of the most important parts of the project.

Implement a modular HTR/OCR engine.

Do not assume that ordinary printed-text OCR can accurately recognize historical handwritten manuscripts.

The system must support historical Persian and Arabic handwriting and Islamic calligraphic styles through a model-adapter architecture.

### 5.1 Model Architecture

Create a common abstract interface:

HTRModel:
- load_model()
- predict(image)
- predict_batch(images)
- get_model_info()
- is_loaded()

Implement a model registry that allows switching between different HTR models through environment variables.

The first supported implementation should use a compatible pretrained vision-encoder/text-decoder architecture, such as TrOCR, when a suitable checkpoint is available.

If the selected checkpoint is not trained for Persian or Arabic handwriting, clearly identify this limitation.

Do not claim language support based only on the architecture.

Provide a clean adapter interface for future custom models trained specifically on Persian and Arabic manuscripts.

The system must support:
- CPU inference
- CUDA GPU inference when available
- Configurable batch size
- Configurable maximum output length
- Model device selection
- Lazy model loading
- Model caching

Do not download a huge model automatically during every application startup.

### 5.2 Image Preprocessing

Use OpenCV and Pillow.

Implement:
- Image validation
- Grayscale conversion
- Noise reduction
- Contrast enhancement
- Adaptive thresholding
- Deskewing
- Resolution normalization
- Optional background removal
- Optional line segmentation

Preprocessing must be configurable.

Keep the original image unchanged.

Store preprocessing settings in recognition metadata.

Do not force aggressive binarization on every image because historical manuscripts may contain delicate ink and paper details.

### 5.3 Text Segmentation

Create a segmentation module that can:

- Detect text regions.
- Detect text lines when possible.
- Preserve reading order.
- Return bounding boxes.
- Support right-to-left reading order.
- Handle multiple lines and columns.

If reliable segmentation is not available, the system should support whole-page inference through the model adapter.

Do not fabricate bounding boxes.

### 5.4 Recognition Output

Return a structured result:

{
  "job_id": "uuid",
  "status": "COMPLETED",
  "text": "recognized manuscript text",
  "language": "fa",
  "model": {
    "name": "model-name",
    "version": "model-version"
  },
  "confidence": null,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "recognized line",
      "confidence": null,
      "bounding_box": null
    }
  ],
  "warnings": [],
  "processing_time_ms": 0
}

Confidence must only be returned when the model provides a meaningful confidence calculation.

Do not invent confidence percentages.

### 5.5 Persian and Arabic Normalization

Implement a dedicated normalization utility.

Handle:
- Arabic and Persian Yeh variants.
- Arabic and Persian Kaf variants.
- Tatweel.
- Diacritics.
- Unicode normalization.
- Repeated whitespace.
- Arabic presentation forms where appropriate.

Preserve the original transcription.

Maintain two text representations:
1. Original text for historical fidelity.
2. Normalized text for search and matching.

Normalization must be configurable because diacritics and letter variations may be important in manuscript research.

Do not silently remove meaningful characters from the original text.

